WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:06.000
The reasons behind their exclusions may vary,&nbsp;
but here's a look at some DCEU characters who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:11.520
all share a common thread: disappearing from&nbsp;
a superhero mega-franchise without a trace.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.520 --> 00:00:14.440
These are the characters who&nbsp;
disappeared without explanation.

00:00:15.440 --> 00:00:18.480
Will Smith's Floyd Lawton, also known as Deadshot,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.480 --> 00:00:23.360
is introduced in Suicide Squad as a master&nbsp;
marksman who never misses his target.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.360 --> 00:00:29.120
He's also shown to be a devoted father to his&nbsp;
daughter, Zoe. After being incarcerated, Deadshot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.120 --> 00:00:34.560
is recruited as a member of the government-run&nbsp;
Suicide Squad team. From there, he helps defeat&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.560 --> 00:00:39.520
the film's nefarious villain Enchantress,&nbsp;
before being put back in his prison cell.

00:00:39.520 --> 00:00:44.000
Originally, this was to be just the&nbsp;
start of Deadshot's journey in the DCEU.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.640 --> 00:00:49.760
Five months before Suicide Squad premiered,&nbsp;
Variety even reported that Smith was reprising&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.760 --> 00:00:54.160
his role as Deadshot in a sequel to&nbsp;
the movie. By the end of that year,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.160 --> 00:01:00.880
various reports noted that a spinoff movie about&nbsp;
Deadshot was in development. In 2018, Smith said:

00:01:00.880 --> 00:01:04.160
"They've been talking about it.&nbsp;
So if a good idea comes around,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.160 --> 00:01:08.400
we've been having meetings. But I&nbsp;
would love to. I love Deadshot."

00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:12.160
In the years since, however, there's been&nbsp;
little word about Deadshot having any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.160 --> 00:01:17.280
further presence in the DCEU. Reflecting&nbsp;
his diminished status in this franchise,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.280 --> 00:01:21.760
Deadshot wasn't even referenced in&nbsp;
Harley Quinn's solo movie, Birds of Prey.

00:01:21.760 --> 00:01:26.480
However, there's still hope for the character's&nbsp;
return. According to Variety, after initial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.480 --> 00:01:31.760
attempts to recast the role, Deadshot was written&nbsp;
out of the upcoming movie The Suicide Squad.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.760 --> 00:01:35.680
But this isn't quite as bad as&nbsp;
it sounds: According to sources,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.680 --> 00:01:40.240
the decision was made in order to allow Smith&nbsp;
the chance to reprise his role in the future.

00:01:40.960 --> 00:01:45.840
For a good while, it appeared that Deathstroke&nbsp;
was poised to be a major fixture in the DCEU.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.480 --> 00:01:52.240
Joe Manganiello was cast in the role in September&nbsp;
2016, intended to be the villain of Ben Affleck's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.240 --> 00:01:58.080
The Batman. A year later, in October 2017,&nbsp;
Gareth Evans was in talks to write and direct&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.080 --> 00:02:03.280
a solo Deathstroke movie starring Manganiello,&nbsp;
too. All of these developments occurred before&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.280 --> 00:02:08.560
Deathstroke made his first DCEU appearance&nbsp;
in a post-credits scene for Justice League,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.560 --> 00:02:12.800
which teased him teaming up with Lex&nbsp;
Luthor to found the Legion of Doom.

00:02:13.520 --> 00:02:19.440
Unfortunately, none of these plans for Deathstroke&nbsp;
ever came to fruition. First, Affleck's version&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.440 --> 00:02:24.480
of The Batman fell apart. Matt Reeves took over&nbsp;
the project and put together a new cast, which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.480 --> 00:02:30.080
didn't include Manganiello. Additionally, the&nbsp;
Deathstroke solo movie never got off the ground.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.080 --> 00:02:36.080
In April 2020, Evans described his proposed&nbsp;
Deathstroke movie to Yahoo! Movies UK as:

00:02:36.080 --> 00:02:40.320
"A lean story, that would be kind of an origin&nbsp;
of that character. Something that felt like it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.320 --> 00:02:46.000
could be 100 minutes or 110 minutes long, max&nbsp;
— not to go over the two hour period with it."

00:02:46.640 --> 00:02:50.560
Plans for another Justice League&nbsp;
movie also never came to fruition,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.560 --> 00:02:55.600
leading to no follow-up on that Deathstroke&nbsp;
post-credits scene. Currently, there are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.600 --> 00:03:01.840
no plans to have Deathstroke show up in&nbsp;
any future DCEU movies. In October 2019,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.840 --> 00:03:07.360
Manganiello referenced his troubled experiences&nbsp;
with Deathstroke by telling Comic Book Resources:

00:03:07.360 --> 00:03:10.960
"Someday I'll write a book about&nbsp;
it, but that time is... yeah."

00:03:12.240 --> 00:03:18.400
Among the military personnel fixated on Superman&nbsp;
in Man of Steel was General Calvin Swanwick.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.400 --> 00:03:24.400
Played by Harry Lennix, Swanwick proved a useful&nbsp;
ally to Superman in the fight against General Zod.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.400 --> 00:03:28.320
In his second appearance in Batman&nbsp;
v. Superman: Dawn of Justice,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.320 --> 00:03:33.120
the character had gone from being a military&nbsp;
general to United States Secretary of Defense.

00:03:33.680 --> 00:03:37.760
Lennix's prominence in the franchise&nbsp;
even extended off the screen.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.760 --> 00:03:42.880
At the 2013 San Diego International&nbsp;
Comic-Con, Zack Snyder had Lennix read&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.880 --> 00:03:48.160
a passage from The Dark Knight Returns as a way&nbsp;
of announcing that the follow-up to Man of Steel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.160 --> 00:03:53.520
would pit Batman against Superman. Snyder also&nbsp;
planned to have Swanwick become a superhero&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.520 --> 00:03:58.960
himself in Justice League. Per the storyboards&nbsp;
that Zack Snyder shared on his Vero account,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.960 --> 00:04:03.600
Swanwick was to eventually be revealed&nbsp;
as the cosmic being Martian Manhunter.

00:04:03.600 --> 00:04:08.640
These plans, however, never came to pass.&nbsp;
After Snyder was taken off Justice League,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.640 --> 00:04:14.080
Swanwick was left out of the film's theatrical&nbsp;
cut. Swanwick hasn't been seen since Batman v.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.080 --> 00:04:18.880
Superman and no future DCEU movies have&nbsp;
announced plans to bring the character back.

00:04:19.840 --> 00:04:23.440
In 2013's Man of Steel, Laurence&nbsp;
Fishburne appeared as the most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.440 --> 00:04:27.520
recent cinematic incarnation of&nbsp;
the Daily Planet chief Perry White.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.520 --> 00:04:31.600
For his take on the character,&nbsp;
Fishburne told ComicBook.com that

00:04:31.600 --> 00:04:36.320
"Essentially, I used my model, my&nbsp;
inspiration really is the late Ed Bradley,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.320 --> 00:04:39.680
who was a CBS correspondent&nbsp;
on 60 Minutes for many years."

00:04:40.240 --> 00:04:45.040
Fishburne reprised the role in the second&nbsp;
entry in Batman v. Superman: Dawn of Justice.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.600 --> 00:04:51.120
Since that 2016 feature, though, Fishburne's&nbsp;
Perry White has been absent from the DCEU.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.120 --> 00:04:55.920
This is partially due to scheduling conflicts on&nbsp;
the part of Fishburne. When asked to reprise the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.920 --> 00:05:01.280
White role in Justice League, Fishburne declined&nbsp;
the offer. He later told The Los Angeles Times:

00:05:01.280 --> 00:05:05.040
"They asked me to come for a day, but&nbsp;
I couldn't work it out, schedule-wise.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.040 --> 00:05:08.160
And really, what do you need the&nbsp;
newspaperman in Justice League for?"

00:05:08.800 --> 00:05:13.440
Fishburne subsequently noted that he&nbsp;
wasn't sure about his future in the DCEU.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.440 --> 00:05:16.240
In 2018, he revealed to Den of Geek:

00:05:16.240 --> 00:05:20.320
"I don't think I'm still on call. I was&nbsp;
unfortunately unable to go do some work&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.320 --> 00:05:24.560
on what turned out to be the Justice League&nbsp;
movie. I don't know that I need to go back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.560 --> 00:05:28.160
or if they'll have me back or not, but I&nbsp;
was grateful to have been a part of it."

00:05:28.720 --> 00:05:32.480
Though a new Superman adventure could&nbsp;
see the character returning, for now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.480 --> 00:05:36.080
the DCEU's Perry White appears&nbsp;
to have become yesterday's news.

00:05:37.520 --> 00:05:41.760
Killer Croc made his live-action movie debut&nbsp;
in Suicide Squad, in which he was portrayed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.760 --> 00:05:47.520
by Adewale Akinnuoye-Agbaje. A member of the&nbsp;
titular group of supervillains, Croc managed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.520 --> 00:05:53.120
to survive the events of Suicide Squad and ended&nbsp;
the film with both a reduced prison sentence and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.120 --> 00:05:59.280
access to BET in his jail cell. However, unlike&nbsp;
some of his fellow Suicide Squad characters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.280 --> 00:06:03.600
Killer Croc will not be returning for the&nbsp;
next installment in the franchise. But this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.600 --> 00:06:08.480
development seems to suit Akinnuoye-Agbaje&nbsp;
just fine. He told Yahoo! Movies UK:

00:06:09.040 --> 00:06:11.680
"I don't think I need to&nbsp;
[do it again], to be honest.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.680 --> 00:06:15.920
I did it, I enjoyed it, it bought me&nbsp;
a Bentley and I will keep moving on."

00:06:15.920 --> 00:06:20.800
Killer Croc being alive at the end of Suicide&nbsp;
Squad means the door is still open for his return,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.800 --> 00:06:25.840
however, should any subsequent DCEU movies&nbsp;
want to further explore the character.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.840 --> 00:06:31.200
For now, though, neither the DCEU&nbsp;
nor Adewale Akinnuoye-Agbaje is in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.200 --> 00:06:34.520
a rush to revisit the scaliest&nbsp;
member of the Suicide Squad.

00:06:35.520 --> 00:06:39.600
"This is Katana. She's got my&nbsp;
back. She could cut all you in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.600 --> 00:06:42.640
half with one sword stroke,&nbsp;
just like mowing the lawn."

00:06:43.520 --> 00:06:47.200
Ok, yeah, not the greatest intro in the world.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.200 --> 00:06:51.680
But Katana showed some pretty&nbsp;
extraordinary superpowers in Suicide Squad,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.680 --> 00:06:55.840
especially when compared to the more&nbsp;
grounded skills of some of her teammates.

00:06:55.840 --> 00:07:00.960
Even with those abilities, though, Katana ended&nbsp;
up having a pretty small role in the movie.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.960 --> 00:07:05.680
This is primarily due to a number of her scenes&nbsp;
ending up on the cutting room floor, including&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.680 --> 00:07:10.480
a sequence during which she became possessed by&nbsp;
Enchantress and attacked the other Suicide Squad&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.480 --> 00:07:16.400
members. Sadly, her reduced presence in Suicide&nbsp;
Squad ended up mirroring her subsequent role in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.400 --> 00:07:23.680
the entire DCEU franchise. Since her debut, Katana&nbsp;
has been absent from all other DCEU properties,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.680 --> 00:07:29.040
including her team's follow-up movie. This is&nbsp;
despite actress Karen Fukuhara having a clear&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.040 --> 00:07:34.640
vision of where she wanted to take the character&nbsp;
in future movies. As she once told ComicBook.com:

00:07:34.640 --> 00:07:36.640
"I'd like to explore where she's from,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.640 --> 00:07:41.280
what kind of training she comes from — if she&nbsp;
gets a stand-alone movie that would be awesome!&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.280 --> 00:07:44.960
There's so much story from the point of&nbsp;
her husband's death to her story leading&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.960 --> 00:07:48.960
up to Suicide Squad — that's something&nbsp;
that I'd love to show on the screen."

00:07:48.960 --> 00:07:52.000
Unfortunately, though she&nbsp;
may have Rick Flagg's back,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.000 --> 00:07:55.840
the DCEU doesn't have any plans&nbsp;
to bring Katana back just yet.

00:07:56.720 --> 00:08:02.320
There are many superheroes in the DCEU, but&nbsp;
none have the technological prowess of Cyborg.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.320 --> 00:08:06.800
He made his first full-fledged appearance as&nbsp;
part of the titular superhero team in Justice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.800 --> 00:08:12.720
League after being introduced to audiences&nbsp;
via a cameo in Batman v. Superman. Originally,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.720 --> 00:08:18.000
the DCEU had big plans for Cyborg which&nbsp;
included the character co-starring in an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.000 --> 00:08:23.440
early version of the Flash movie. From there,&nbsp;
Cyborg was supposed to get his own solo movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.440 --> 00:08:27.360
entitled Cyborg, which was set&nbsp;
for an April 3rd 2020 release.

00:08:28.400 --> 00:08:28.960
"Booyah."

00:08:29.840 --> 00:08:35.040
However, those ambitions ultimately went&nbsp;
unfulfilled. Since Justice League in 2017,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.040 --> 00:08:40.160
Cyborg has made no further appearances&nbsp;
in the DCEU. Ray Fisher has said the lack&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.160 --> 00:08:45.280
of movement for the solo Cyborg movie is&nbsp;
simply due to money. He told CinemaBlend:

00:08:45.280 --> 00:08:48.800
"There's a ton of people making those&nbsp;
kinds of decisions behind the scenes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.800 --> 00:08:54.160
And it would be a very, very costly movie to&nbsp;
make because it is so CGI heavy with Cyborg.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.160 --> 00:08:57.840
Because I'm the only member of the&nbsp;
League that's not in a practical costume.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.840 --> 00:09:00.880
You're going to be looking at&nbsp;
$200 million just to make it."

00:09:00.880 --> 00:09:03.840
Despite the character's absence from the DCEU,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.840 --> 00:09:06.880
Fisher hasn't given up hope&nbsp;
on playing Cyborg again.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.880 --> 00:09:11.920
Fisher commented on the prospect of reprising&nbsp;
the role in a Twitch livestream by saying:

00:09:11.920 --> 00:09:14.400
"Nothing's off the table [...] Knock on wood."

00:09:14.400 --> 00:09:16.880
With no future appearances on the horizon, though,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.880 --> 00:09:21.840
the most tech-savvy DCEU hero has,&nbsp;
for now, been put in sleep mode.

00:09:22.640 --> 00:09:26.320
Prior to the release of Suicide&nbsp;
Squad, theories abounded about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.320 --> 00:09:32.400
who Ike Barinholtz was playing in the film. Dr.&nbsp;
Hugo Strange was a particularly popular guess.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.400 --> 00:09:36.640
But if you're struggling to remember if&nbsp;
Barinholtz was even in Suicide Squad,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.640 --> 00:09:40.720
well, it's because he wasn't playing&nbsp;
an famous comic book character at all.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.720 --> 00:09:46.560
In reality, Barinholtz ended up playing a totally&nbsp;
original character by the name of Hunter Griggs.

00:09:46.560 --> 00:09:51.440
Griggs was a sadistic prison guard at Belle&nbsp;
Reeve tasked with guarding the supervillains&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.440 --> 00:09:56.240
that would make up the Suicide Squad team. In&nbsp;
the earliest scenes of the film, he's shown&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.240 --> 00:10:02.240
perpetrating particularly nasty acts of abuse&nbsp;
against Deadshot and Harley Quinn. Eventually,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.240 --> 00:10:07.920
Griggs is taken captive by the Joker, but at this&nbsp;
point he pretty much vanishes from the movie.

00:10:07.920 --> 00:10:10.080
"You're so screwed."

00:10:10.080 --> 00:10:14.480
"What do you mean by that? What do you mean&nbsp;
by that? Harley, what do you mean by that?"

00:10:15.280 --> 00:10:20.240
The audience is never shown Griggs' fate,&nbsp;
and the DCEU is apparently in no hurry to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:20.240 --> 00:10:25.120
clarify Harley's statement. Since Suicide&nbsp;
Squad, Griggs has failed to appear in any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.120 --> 00:10:29.840
other DCEU properties and he isn't a part&nbsp;
of the cast of The Suicide Squad. Though&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:29.840 --> 00:10:34.960
pre-release speculation ran rampant over who&nbsp;
Hunter Griggs was, it turns out the character&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:34.960 --> 00:10:40.160
wasn't all that important to Suicide Squad&nbsp;
— or, for that matter, the DCEU at large.

00:10:41.280 --> 00:10:47.040
Batman v. Superman: Dawn of Justice was all&nbsp;
about showdowns between superheroes. But it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:47.040 --> 00:10:53.200
still had room for a supervillain. That adversary&nbsp;
was Superman foe Lex Luthor, here portrayed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:53.200 --> 00:10:58.640
by Jesse Eisenberg. The man who orchestrated&nbsp;
the titular duel between Batman and Superman,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:58.640 --> 00:11:04.320
Luthor is also responsible for creating Doomsday,&nbsp;
the creature that eventually kills Superman.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:04.320 --> 00:11:10.080
By the end of Batman v. Superman, Luthor has been&nbsp;
sent to prison, had his head shaved, and delivered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:10.080 --> 00:11:15.360
some vague warnings to Batman indicating that&nbsp;
he's aware of an impending attack by Darkseid.

00:11:15.360 --> 00:11:20.400
Luthor's final moments in Batman v. Superman set&nbsp;
up plenty of different directions the character&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:20.400 --> 00:11:26.240
could go down in future DCEU movies. However,&nbsp;
despite those cliffhangers, the DCEU version&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:26.240 --> 00:11:30.800
of Luthor and his potential connections to&nbsp;
Darkseid have been almost totally ignored:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:30.800 --> 00:11:36.240
He's only appeared once since Batman v.&nbsp;
Superman, in a Justice League post-credits scene.

00:11:36.240 --> 00:11:41.840
"To put it plainly, shouldn't&nbsp;
we have a league of our own?"

00:11:42.800 --> 00:11:47.920
In the years since Justice League, no other&nbsp;
DCEU movies have even referenced this particular&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:47.920 --> 00:11:52.880
supervillain. And with no upcoming DCEU movies&nbsp;
planning to end the character's absence,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:52.880 --> 00:11:58.160
even Eisenberg is doubtful he'll ever return&nbsp;
to the role of Lex Luthor. As he told MovieWeb:

00:11:58.160 --> 00:12:00.960
"I probably won't do another. I don't know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:00.960 --> 00:12:03.840
if they're making another&nbsp;
one, where I would be in it."

